06","marc:marc_nuls/NULS_PHC_180925.mrc:268748514:2015","idb:9780262541152","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-006.mrc:243595533:3195"],"title":"Out of the Crisis","notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Source title: Out of the Crisis (The MIT Press)"},"number_of_pages":524,"isbn_13":["9780262541152"],"isbn_10":["0262541157"],"publish_date":"Aug 11, 2000","works":[{"key":"/works/OL3190937W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"ocaid":"outofcrisis0000demi","lccn":["2001268898","00042729"],"local_id":["urn:bwbsku:O7-CUR-292","urn:nuls:31786101644018"],"oclc_numbers":["47060282","254639936"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"According to W. Edwards Deming, American companies require nothing less than a transformation of management style and of governmental relations with industry. In Out of the Crisis, originally published in 1986, Deming offers a theory of management based on his 14 Points for Management. Management's failure to plan for the future, he claims, brings about loss of market, which brings about loss of jobs.\n\nManagement must be judged not only by the quarterly dividend, but by innovative plans to stay in business, protect investment, ensure future dividends, and provide more jobs through improved product and service. In simple direct language, he explains the principles of management transformation and how to apply them.\"--BOOK JACKET."},"latest_revision":9,"revision":9,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2020-04-30T16:20:47.795350"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-06-07T00:02:16.733589"}}},"OLID:OL29386061M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL29386061M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL29386061M/Out_of_the_Crisis_Reissue","preview":"noview","preview_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL29386061M/Out_of_the_Crisis_Reissue","details":{"publishers":["MIT Press","The MIT Press"],"source_records":["bwb:9780262535946","marc:marc_nuls/NULS_PHC_180925.mrc:196053898:2231","idb:9780262535946"],"title":"Out of the Cris